hwrng: rockchip: the RK3399's TRNG, in its crypto block

Khadas Edge1, Android 14 on mainline 6.12. Applied to kernel/mainline by
build/build-kernel.sh before every build (device/khadas/edge/kernel/patches/).

6.12's rockchip-rng knows only the RK3568, whose TRNG is a block of its own.
The RK3399 has no such block: its TRNG is part of each crypto engine (the
RK3288 one, "crypto v1"), at 0x200 in the engine's registers, started from
the engine's CTRL. Rockchip's own kernel drives it as rockchip,cryptov1-rng.
With nothing to bind to, the board has no /dev/hw_random, and Android's
prng_seeder, which seeds userspace from that device, fails at every boot.

A second variant here, "rockchip,rk3399-crypto-rng", with Rockchip's
register sequence: osc ring on with a sample period of 100, CTRL's RNG_START
(write-masked in the upper half), poll until it clears, read 256 bits from
TRNG_DOUT_0..7. The RK3568 path is unchanged, moved behind match data.

Quality 500: not measured on the RK3399 (Rockchip's kernel claims 999), so
half is credited; rng_quality in sysfs changes it. Patch 0010 adds the node.

